{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"**U.S. iShares example (bank lending agent):** In a securities loan, the borrower must return the ETF’s securities; collateral is held to secure that obligation. If the borrower defaults, the fund may need to liquidate collateral and buy back the lent securities. Losses can remain if collateral is insufficient, illiquid, or hard to realize. Controls typically include borrower credit limits, collateral haircuts and daily marking, diversification, and contractual default remedies. These reduce—not eliminate—loss risk.\n\n**Keep the risks distinct:**\n- **Borrower/default risk:** borrower fails to return securities. Controls include counterparty due diligence, limits, and collateral.\n- **Collateral-shortfall risk:** collateral value falls below the replacement cost, or cannot be liquidated promptly. Controls include overcollateralization, haircuts, frequent valuation, and margin calls; gaps can still occur.\n- **Cash-reinvestment risk:** when cash is posted as collateral, reinvestment can lose value or become illiquid, leaving a shortfall when cash must be returned. Controls include conservative investment guidelines, liquidity limits, and monitoring.\n\n**UCITS rules:** UCITS securities-lending arrangements generally require the fund to be able to terminate and recall lent securities at any time. Collateral must meet regulatory eligibility, liquidity, valuation, diversification, and custody requirements. These are UCITS requirements, not a blanket description of the U.S. iShares bank example—and neither regime makes collateral a guarantee against loss."}}
